\begin{proof}
Every element of $\mathbb{Z}[i]/(q)$ is represented by some $a+bi$ with $0\le a,b<q$, by reducing both integer coefficients modulo $q$. If two such representatives determine the same coset, then $q$ divides both coefficient differences, so $a_1=a_2$ and $b_1=b_2$. Thus the quotient has exactly $q^2$ elements.

Since $q\equiv3\pmod4$, $q$ is irreducible in $\mathbb{Z}[i]$, hence prime because $\mathbb{Z}[i]$ is a Euclidean domain. Therefore $(q)$ is a prime ideal, and the quotient $\mathbb{Z}[i]/(q)$ is an integral domain. Every finite integral domain is a field: for any nonzero element, multiplication by it is injective and therefore surjective, so it has a multiplicative inverse. Hence $\mathbb{Z}[i]/(q)$ is a field with $q^2$ elements.
\end{proof}
